  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a potential mechanism by which Dihydroartemisinate may interact with VMRC-MELG, through a series of molecular targets that are all implicated in the biological process of SNU-387. Dihydroartemisinate is shown to have a direct target of SNU-387, which is itself a target of multiple compounds, including Quercetagetin 3-methyl ether 7-glucoside and 5-Hydroxy-6,8-Dimethoxy-2,3-Dimethylbenzo[G]Chromen-4-One. These compounds are all linked to VMRC-MELG, indicating a possible pathway where Dihydroartemisinate may influence the activity of SNU-387, which in turn modulates VMRC-MELG. The evidence suggests that Dihydroartemisinate could act as a modulator of SNU-387, potentially altering its interaction with VMRC-MELG. The mechanism is supported by the presence of a direct interaction between Dihydroartemisinate and SNU-387, and the subsequent targeting of VMRC-MELG by multiple compounds that share a common target. The integration of these pathways implies that Dihydroartemisinate may exert its effects through a mechanism involving SNU-387, which could be a key mediator in the interaction with VMRC-MELG.",
